# SHINOBU_36 Rationale

Date: 2026-05-18
Domain: INPUT_DETERMINISM_AND_HAPTICS
Status: PENDING UNITY VERIFICATION - DOTNET TARGETS GREEN

## R6 - Active State Recovery After Archive

Problem: Active `Docs/Tasks/Status_SHINOBU_36.md`, `Docs/AgentLogs/Rationale_SHINOBU_36.md`, and `Docs/Tasks/CURRENT_BATCH.md` disappeared because batch hygiene moved prior files into `Docs/Archive/Batch008`; cleanup removed the recreated active files twice during verification.

Solution: Recreated current active state files and recorded only fresh continuation evidence. Previous batch files remain archived.

Rejected Alternatives: Treating archived files as active current-batch truth was rejected. Chat-only memory was rejected because context compression is unsafe.

Scalability potential: No runtime cost. It protects coordination from weak laptop to high-end workstation.

Hardware Impact: 0 us runtime gain; prevents repeated compile/verification churn on i3/MX350 developer hardware.

## R7 - External Compile Guard Repair

Problem: `Hecton8.Core.csproj` exposed external compile failures after input was fixed: `TerminalOsTypes.cs` referenced a non-imported `ISignal`, `WakeRequestSignal` existed but was not included in the core csproj, and `SomaticKinematicsRuntime.cs` expected `PhysicsDeterminismSignals`/`EnsureNativeState(bool)`.

Solution: Fully qualified the TerminalOS signal marker, included `PhysicsWakeSignalContracts.cs` in `Hecton8.Core.csproj`, added `using Hecton8.Physics`, and restored the bool overload so cold initialization is blocked from `FixedTick`.

Rejected Alternatives: Dummy signal shims and broad runtime rewrites were rejected. They would hide real contract drift and cross domain boundaries.

Scalability potential: Low tier avoids accidental cold file/archive work in hot KCC ticks; high/ultra keep the same signal corridor.

Hardware Impact: Worst-case spike prevention. Average frame gain is not measurable locally without Unity profiler.

## R8 - Full Button Mask Closure

Problem: Earlier bitmask packing covered core gameplay buttons but not every boolean action cached by the InputSystem owner. UI-map buttons could be invisible in `InputStateDTO` when Player map was disabled.

Solution: Expanded `PlayerInputAction` with PDA, Inventory, Cancel, TabNext, TabPrevious, ToolSlot1-4, Flashlight, and Pause. `BuildPolledButtonMask()` packs every cached boolean action, and `CaptureState()` now builds the mask whenever `InputManager` exists while keeping analog movement/look gated by Player map.

Rejected Alternatives: `WasPressedThisFrame` edge calls were rejected as a second authority path. UI-specific spaghetti checks in movement were rejected; `InputBlockMask` remains the deterministic control lock.

Scalability potential: Low = one 32-bit mask, 10-frame window. Middle = same mask feeds UI/gameplay. High/Ultra = replay/oscilloscope/debug reads use the same truth without extra gameplay polling.

Hardware Impact: Sub-us OR chain. Main gain is removing replay desync and rollback repair cost.

## R9 - Verification Boundary

Problem: Full `Assembly-CSharp.csproj` cannot compile because `RealtimeCSG.csproj` references missing source files under `Assets/RealtimeCSG`, a third-party package wall outside input ownership.

Solution: Classified the wall and kept SHINOBU verification on first-party target assemblies: Core, Input, and Editor. Static scans cover legacy input/callback/Pack=1 constraints in the SHINOBU path.

Rejected Alternatives: Creating fake RealtimeCSG files or editing third-party csproj entries was rejected under third-party asset integrity rules.

Scalability potential: No runtime effect.

Hardware Impact: 0 us runtime gain; avoids vendor contamination and unnecessary rebuild churn.
